WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.017
Welcome, instructors!

2
00:00:02.017 --> 00:00:07.970
In the Chronicle Fundamentals lab, you will guide participants—from curious

3
00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:13.448
kids to first-time Copilot builders—from a completely empty folder to

4
00:00:13.448 --> 00:00:18.131
a tested, zero-dependency Node.js web app in sixty minutes.

5
00:00:18.131 --> 00:00:22.973
Because learners finish by opening their own real VS Code and

6
00:00:22.973 --> 00:00:29.958
Copilot Agent sessions at http://127.0.0.1:3210, the payoff feels magical—and our strict

7
00:00:29.958 --> 00:00:36.150
zero-package design eliminates classroom npm and firewall failures completely.

8
00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:41.292
Ten minutes before doors open, lock in four pre-flight checks.

9
00:00:41.292 --> 00:00:45.398
First, keep a finished Chronicle server running on port 3210

10
00:00:45.398 --> 00:00:48.819
so you can demo the finish line in ninety seconds.

11
00:00:48.819 --> 00:00:54.088
Second, verify Node twenty-two or newer and make sure everyone opens workshop

12
00:00:54.088 --> 00:00:59.083
slash Fundamentals slash sampleapp directly as their VS Code root folder.

13
00:00:59.083 --> 00:01:03.462
Third, have students pick a frontier model at standard or Medium

14
00:01:03.462 --> 00:01:09.073
reasoning effort—High reasoning can stretch a three-minute build past ten minutes!

15
00:01:09.073 --> 00:01:15.300
And fourth, suggest Assisted or Allow all permissions inside their isolated starter folder.

16
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:21.888
Keep a brisk, celebratory rhythm across five timed checkpoints: seven minutes for

17
00:01:21.888 --> 00:01:26.874
Lab Zero setup, eight minutes for the Lab One rulebook, fifteen minutes

18
00:01:26.874 --> 00:01:31.930
for Lab Two's dual-format parser, Myers diff engine, and unit tests, ten

19
00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:36.987
minutes for Lab Three's loopback http server, and twenty minutes for Lab

20
00:01:36.987 --> 00:01:41.200
Four's browser interface and five-minute live treasure hunt.

21
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:46.811
While Copilot spends two to four minutes generating code in Labs Two

22
00:01:46.811 --> 00:01:52.007
through Four, fill the air with visual stories rather than syntax lectures!

23
00:01:52.007 --> 00:01:57.134
Right after Lab One, have every student click the References toggle at the

24
00:01:57.134 --> 00:02:04.894
top of Copilot's reply so they actually see dot-github slash copilot-instructions-dot-md attached automatically.

25
00:02:04.894 --> 00:02:09.467
Model the core engineering loop on every lab: Prompt clearly, Peek

26
00:02:09.467 --> 00:02:13.000
at the generated files, and run npm test to verify!

27
00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.513
Here are your fastest classroom rescues.

28
00:02:16.513 --> 00:02:20.954
First, if Copilot ignores the zero-package rule and tries to run npm

29
00:02:20.954 --> 00:02:25.983
install, glance at the student's Explorer sidebar—nine times out of ten, they

30
00:02:25.983 --> 00:02:31.012
opened the parent repository instead of opening sampleapp as the root folder!

31
00:02:31.012 --> 00:02:33.429
Have them re-open sampleapp directly.

32
00:02:33.429 --> 00:02:37.805
Second, if a turn seems stuck with a spinning indicator, scroll the

33
00:02:37.805 --> 00:02:42.050
Chat pane to click any waiting Allow or Keep confirmation button.

34
00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.860
On developer laptops, watch for two subtle platform quirks

35
00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.961
we already guarded against in the lab prompts.

36
00:02:49.961 --> 00:02:55.556
On Windows, server tests should write temporary static fixtures to os.tmpdir rather

37
00:02:55.556 --> 00:03:01.286
than inside the workspace public folder, preventing VS Code file-watcher EBUSY locks.

38
00:03:01.286 --> 00:03:05.668
And if an advanced attendee has Chrome DevTools or Playwright MCP

39
00:03:05.668 --> 00:03:10.792
servers installed globally, remind them to validate Lab Four generation with

40
00:03:10.792 --> 00:03:16.050
node dash-dash-check first rather than spinning up browser MCP tools mid-turn!

41
00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:21.269
If a participant joins late or hits a network hiccup in Lab Two

42
00:03:21.269 --> 00:03:26.616
or Three, you have a built-in golden safety net: workshop slash Advanced slash

43
00:03:26.616 --> 00:03:32.306
sampleapp contains a clean, tested snapshot of the exact finished Fundamentals app!

44
00:03:32.306 --> 00:03:37.036
You can copy any missing backend file into their folder in seconds so

45
00:03:37.036 --> 00:03:42.177
everyone builds the Lab Four UI and celebrates at the finish line together.

46
00:03:42.177 --> 00:03:47.867
And when projecting on a bright room screen, toggle Chronicle's high-contrast White

47
00:03:47.867 --> 00:03:51.500
Mode so every row is crystal clear from the back row!

48
00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:59.254
Save the final five minutes for an energizing group treasure hunt at http://127.0.0.1:3210!

49
00:03:59.254 --> 00:04:05.355
Have learners click Agent sessions to inspect the very workshop conversation they

50
00:04:05.355 --> 00:04:09.347
just used to build the app, flip sorting from A to Z,

51
00:04:09.347 --> 00:04:13.791
inspect a green-and-red file diff, and run a global search.

52
00:04:13.791 --> 00:04:20.193
Then close by teasing the ninety-minute Advanced Workshop—where they will evolve this

53
00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:26.294
exact app using an Orchestrator agent, four specialist agents with custom skills,

54
00:04:26.294 --> 00:04:31.717
Microsoft Foundry AI session summaries, and a Windows desktop installer.

55
00:04:31.717 --> 00:04:33.600
Have an awesome delivery!
